GNU Make, colorized
===================

This is GNU Make with colored errors and warnings, built from
https://github.com/XtheOne/make-colorized.  It is not an official GNU
release.  It is based on the development version of GNU Make (4.4.90).

What is different from GNU Make
-------------------------------

* make's own errors are shown in red and its warnings in yellow.

* In the output of recipes, the words "error" and "warning" are shown in
  red and magenta, so compiler and linker problems stand out.

* While colors are on, recipe output is passed through a pipe and shown
  line by line while the recipe runs.  Each line is colored as it arrives.

* The --color option controls this:

    --color=auto     Colors only when the output goes to a terminal (the
                     default).  The environment variable NO_COLOR, or
                     TERM=dumb, turns colors off.
    --color=always   Always color, also when the output goes to a file or a
                     pipe, for example a CI log that shows ANSI colors.
    --color, alone   The same as --color=always.
    --color=never    No colors; the output is exactly that of GNU Make.

  The option can also be set for every run with the MAKEFLAGS environment
  variable, for example MAKEFLAGS=--color=always.

Windows package
---------------

make.exe is a 64-bit build made with Microsoft Visual C++.  Copy it to a
folder on your PATH.  Colors need Windows 10 or later; the Windows console
and Windows Terminal show them.

The executable is not code-signed, so Windows SmartScreen may warn the
first time you run it.

